  "summary": "Hollywood's dark crime style: venetian-blind shadows, smoking interiors, femme fatales and moral fog, shot in high-contrast black and white.",
  "description": "Named by French critics (Nino Frank, 1946), film noir fused German Expressionist lighting, hardboiled fiction and wartime disillusion into detective and crime dramas — The Maltese Falcon, Double Indemnity, The Big Sleep. Its grammar: low-key chiaroscuro, dutch angles, rain-slick streets, voiceover fatalism. Neo-noir (Chinatown, Blade Runner) and global noir keep regenerating it; its lighting is the mother tongue of cinematic darkness.",
